# Runebook

Local markdown notes at `projects/runebook.sh/`.

## Crates

- `crates/runebook` owns the vault, note ids, wiki links, and the in-memory index.
- `crates/runebook-app` owns the GPUI window. Do not put file rules in the app crate.

## Vault

- A vault root contains `runebook.toml` (`schema_version = 1`).
- A note is a `*.md` file. `NoteId` is the relative path without `.md`, with `/` separators.
- A note has one kind: Book, Speech, Sight, Deed, or Research.
- Book is a markdown file with no header. A file with no header is a Book note.
- Speech, Sight, Deed, and Research start with this header. `kind` is `speech`, `sight`, `deed`, or `research`.

```markdown
---
kind: speech
---
```

- The header must be the first lines, inside the first 256 bytes. Any other header stays in the body, and the note is Book.
- The editor shows the body. Save writes the header back from the open note's kind.
- Speech stores a transcript. The app does not save the audio file.
- Sight stores a markdown link. The mp4 sits beside that note.
- Deed is a markdown checklist.
- Research stores a question. Charlotte writes a plan, gathers any missing context, then investigates. The note keeps the plan and the findings. The report stays on screen. The source switch stays hidden.
- A preview checkbox toggles that task line in the note.
- A media file beside a note uses a video, audio, or image extension: `mp4`, `webm`, `mkv`, `mov`, `mp3`, `wav`, `ogg`, `m4a`, `flac`, `png`, `jpg`, `jpeg`, `gif`, `webp`.
- Folder delete removes those files. Any other non-note file still blocks folder delete.
- A preview row that is only a video or audio link is an HTML5 `video` or `audio` element with controls. `media_muted` sets `muted`.
- A preview image shows in the row. A click opens a vault file. A remote image loads from an `http` or `https` address. The file must be 8 MiB or smaller.
- The page asks for that file through a vault protocol. The page does not receive a filesystem path.
- The alt text shows when that image does not load. The path must stay inside the vault.
- Skip hidden paths and symlinks. Do not follow a symlink out of the vault.
- There is no sidecar database. The index is rebuilt from the files.
- User config is `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/runebook/config.toml`. It stores `last_vault`, `last_note`, `reopen_last_note`, `microphone`, `speech_model`, `speech_language`, `theme`, `locale`, `text_size`, `note_view`, `line_numbers`, `soft_wrap`, `autosave`, `media_muted`, `dock_layout`, `tool_rounds`, and the model fields. An empty model field uses the matching environment variable.
- `reopen_last_note` opens `last_note` when that note is still in the vault. Otherwise the window opens `welcome`, then the first note.
- `autosave` is `fast` (200 ms), `normal` (400 ms), `slow` (1 s), or `manual`. Manual writes the note when you leave it or close the window.
- `speech_model` is `fast` (`whisper-large-v3-turbo`) or `accurate` (`whisper-large-v3`). An empty `speech_language` lets Groq detect the language. A saved language is a two-letter code.
- `locale` is the window language. `en` is English. A blank or unknown code uses English. Window text lives in `locales/en.json`.
- `text_size` is `small`, `medium`, or `large`. Medium matches the active theme. Line numbers and soft wrap default to on. `media_muted` starts a new clip silent.
- A theme file is a VS Code color theme. `name`, `type`, `colors`, and `tokenColors` are the keys that matter. `type` is `dark` or `light`.
- Built-in themes are `themes/runebook-dark.json`, `themes/runebook-light.json`, `themes/obsidian-dark.json`, `themes/obsidian-light.json`, `themes/blue-topaz-dark.json`, `themes/blue-topaz-light.json`, and the 15 marketplace themes listed in `themes/NOTICE`.
- User themes are `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/runebook/themes/*.json`. When `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is unset, the directory is `~/.config/runebook/themes`.
- `theme` is the theme name. `dark` and `light` still select the built-in themes. An unknown name uses Runebook Dark.
- The app ignores `semanticTokenColors`. An `include` must be one `.json` file in the same folder.
- Font, radius, and note spacing sit in an optional `runebook` object. A missing object uses Noto Sans 14, Noto Sans Mono 13, radius 6 and 8, and the built-in heading scale.

## Links

Resolve `[[target]]` in this order:

1. Exact `NoteId`
2. Unique file stem, only when `target` has no `/`

`[[target|alias]]` shows `alias` and still resolves `target`.

## App

- Call `gpui_kit::init` before any GPUI Kit control.
- The window uses a Kit `TitleBar`. The toolbar sits in that bar.
- Settings in the note area are Appearance, Notes, Capture, and Models, in that order.
- Each settings row has a sentence. The label tooltip adds the default or the environment name.
- Research and the Archivist pick a model from the host list at `/v1/models`. The menu search filters that list. Default leaves that field empty.
- On Linux, the window id is `runebook`. Startup writes `$XDG_DATA_HOME/runebook/icon.png` and `$XDG_DATA_HOME/applications/runebook.desktop`. When `XDG_DATA_HOME` is unset, that directory is `~/.local/share`. The shell uses the desktop file for the book icon.
- On Linux, a Wayland session keeps the window on Wayland. The note page is a second Wayland surface over the preview column, so the type stays sharp. A session with no Wayland display uses an X11 window, and the page is a child of that window. Startup sets `GDK_BACKEND` to the same display server. Screen capture still follows `WAYLAND_DISPLAY`.
- One folder argument opens that folder. No argument opens `last_vault`. A folder with no `runebook.toml` becomes a new vault. Two paths, an empty path, a path that starts with `-`, or a path that is not UTF-8 exits with status 2. A second launch is a second process.
- On macOS, startup writes `~/Library/Services/Open in Runebook.workflow`. Finder shows **Open in Runebook** under Quick Actions for a folder. The service starts the binary with that folder.
- On macOS, a right-click on a folder in the file tree shows **Open in Finder**. That item reveals the folder.
- The file tree, the note, and the Archivist column sit in one horizontal split.
- The file tree reads the vault every 500 ms. A change made outside the app shows up on that read. The refresh button reads immediately.
- A clean open note reloads when its file changed. Unsaved text stays in the editor.
- The Archivist can attach up to 8 files. An image or a PDF goes to the model. UTF-8 text (`txt`, `md`, `csv`, `json`, `html`) stays in the message. Each file must be 8 MiB or smaller. The transcript shows the file name.
- Clear in the Archivist header drops the transcript. The next send starts a new conversation. A turn that is still running does not write its reply.
- Search lists notes whose body contains the query, and a click opens that note.
- A drag stops at the sidebar minimum in `chrome.rs`. The note column stops at `NOTE_MIN_WIDTH`.
- An older layout names the panels `files`, `note`, and `archivist`.
- `dock_layout` stores the files width and the Archivist width. An older dock JSON still supplies those widths. A bad value uses the default split.
- The source pane is the Kit `Editor`. The note preview is one webview page.
- The Archivist column and the research report stay on the Kit text view.
- The page takes its colors, font, radius, and heading scale from the active theme.
- A heading uses the theme heading color. Code uses the theme code color.
- The Kit text view uses the theme syntax colors when a code fence has a grammar.
- HTML in the page is sanitized. A script tag, an embedded frame, and every `on*` attribute are dropped.
- The window shows the preview until the user flips to the source. Source, settings, and the research report hide the page.
- A click selects preview rows. The page paints the range Rust sends back.
- The page menu copies that markdown, adds it to the Archivist, or asks the Archivist to rewrite it.
- The source editor keeps its own selection menu.
- Ctrl+C in the page asks Rust for the markdown.
- Autosave is debounced. Also flush on note switch and window close.
